WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.089
Welcome to the Concilium Daily Global Security Brief for Tuesday, October twentieth.

2
00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:17.479
Your five minute briefing of security developments from the last twenty four hours, sourced from government travel advisories and trusted security analysts, and curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

3
00:00:17.479 --> 00:00:22.843
We name the source for every item, so you can follow up on anything that affects your teams.

4
00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:26.606
Here's what you need to know today.

5
00:00:26.606 --> 00:00:30.991
There are five main items, then a quick regional roundup.

6
00:00:30.991 --> 00:00:32.351
First, and most important for travel, Saudi Arabia.

7
00:00:32.351 --> 00:00:43.564
The State Department travel account says King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh is still shut after missile and drone attacks on airports in the Kingdom.

8
00:00:43.564 --> 00:00:45.880
Jeddah and Dammam are open, but with delays and cancellations.

9
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:52.666
US government employees now need special approval from the Chief of Mission to use any airport in Saudi Arabia.

10
00:00:52.666 --> 00:01:06.239
The advice to US citizens is to expect possible attacks on airports, airspace closures and cancelled flights, to take extreme caution, and to follow Saudi Civil Defense alerts that come through as cell phone broadcasts.

11
00:01:06.239 --> 00:01:15.397
On the same story, the TRAC terrorism account reports the Houthis claiming new ballistic missile and drone attacks on several locations across Saudi Arabia.

12
00:01:15.397 --> 00:01:28.631
The Institute for the Study of War says the Houthis are changing tactics to get around Saudi air defenses, firing different weapons at the same time and going after places without air defense cover.

13
00:01:28.631 --> 00:01:29.675
Second, Ebola.

14
00:01:29.675 --> 00:01:36.851
The World Health Organization says Nigeria has confirmed one imported case of Bundibugyo Ebola in the capital area around Abuja.

15
00:01:36.851 --> 00:01:41.913
The patient is a woman who had recently traveled from Uganda.

16
00:01:41.913 --> 00:01:44.462
She is in isolation, and forty four contacts are being monitored.

17
00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:51.789
Nigeria is the fifth country to detect this virus since the outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo was declared in May.

18
00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:56.389
The WHO is not advising travel restrictions.

19
00:01:56.389 --> 00:02:06.089
Separately, the UK Foreign Office travel advice account updated its Tanzania guidance on Ebola screening, and on a risk of protests on October twenty ninth.

20
00:02:06.089 --> 00:02:06.936
Third, Kenya.

21
00:02:06.936 --> 00:02:14.876
The TRAC terrorism account reports an armed assault by al Shabaab on Christian civilians in Baure, in Lamu County on the coast.

22
00:02:14.876 --> 00:02:23.378
That is one report so far and it has not been confirmed, but it is the kind of incident teams working in Kenya will want to know about.

23
00:02:23.378 --> 00:02:24.539
Fourth, Ethiopia.

24
00:02:24.539 --> 00:02:34.826
The Critical Threats Project says the Tigray People's Liberation Front has threatened to shoot down Ethiopian Airlines planes it believes are carrying military equipment or personnel.

25
00:02:34.826 --> 00:02:48.137
Federal forces are reportedly moving south from the Eritrean border toward Adwa and Adigrat, and rebel groups in Benishangul Gumuz and Oromia have carried out their first notable attacks since joining a new rebel coalition.

26
00:02:48.137 --> 00:02:56.693
Crisis Group warns that Ethiopian and Eritrean forces are sitting very close together, with a real risk of a direct clash.

27
00:02:56.693 --> 00:02:57.785
Fifth, the Sahel.

28
00:02:57.785 --> 00:03:06.786
ACLED, citing Reuters, reports at least forty eight civilians killed in eastern Mali in attacks blamed on Islamic State linked militants.

29
00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:14.919
In Burkina Faso, the TRAC account reports al Qaeda linked JNIM fighters taking over a local militia base in Bam Province.

30
00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:16.589
Now the quick roundup.

31
00:03:16.589 --> 00:03:25.899
In the Middle East, the TRAC account reports that Israeli settlers attacked a visiting French mayor and the Archbishop of Cambrai in Taybeh, in the West Bank.

32
00:03:25.899 --> 00:03:28.953
That one matters for church groups visiting the area.

33
00:03:28.953 --> 00:03:36.737
The Long War Journal reports Iran saying Iraq has agreed to expel Kurdish Iranian opposition groups.

34
00:03:36.737 --> 00:03:51.128
Elsewhere in Africa, there were al Shabaab attacks in Lower Shabelle and near Bosaso in Somalia, M23 clashes in North Kivu in Congo, and an Islamic State West Africa attack on the army in Borno, Nigeria, all from the TRAC account.

35
00:03:51.128 --> 00:04:00.843
In Pakistan, the Pakistani Taliban claim twenty three attacks across Waziristan and nearby districts, and a roadside bomb hit a police vehicle in Quetta.

36
00:04:00.843 --> 00:04:10.738
In Latin America, InSight Crime reports that a gang attack in Croix des Bouquets, Haiti, left four Salvadoran soldiers from the international force missing.

37
00:04:10.738 --> 00:04:20.202
Colombian forces defused a powerful roadside bomb on the Pan American Highway in Cauca, and cartel threat banners appeared in Zacatecas, Mexico.

38
00:04:20.202 --> 00:04:28.315
In Europe, the State Department travel account warns of more protests across Italy after looting and clashes in Milan and Bologna.

39
00:04:28.315 --> 00:04:35.383
Australia's Smartraveller flags Serbia's elections on October twenty fifth, with protests that could turn violent.

40
00:04:35.383 --> 00:04:43.201
On cyber, the cybersecurity agency CISA put out a joint advisory with the FBI on SafePay ransomware.

41
00:04:43.201 --> 00:04:47.817
The US Geological Survey reported two strong quakes off Vanuatu, magnitude six point six and six point three.

42
00:04:47.817 --> 00:04:55.298
And nothing came through on Central Asia or Kyrgyzstan in this window.

43
00:04:55.298 --> 00:04:57.120
That's the brief for today.

44
00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.292
This brief summarizes public reporting and is not a substitute for your organization's own risk assessment.

45
00:05:03.292 --> 00:05:06.696
Check the original source before making travel decisions.

46
00:05:06.696 --> 00:05:08.881
This brief is digitally narrated.

47
00:05:08.881 --> 00:05:14.985
If you have questions, you can always reach out to Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

48
00:05:14.985 --> 00:05:17.312
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
